title Caravan harvest -- readiness converts a slot, never a domain
prompt Autonomous loop -- take the next agent-doable lap Lindy-first crux-first on the Microkernel Target double-seat, Standfast on reds first, one council-rota doc deep-read, condense REMEMBER, send the finished increment.
think Standfast first. Walked crux/REDS.md end to end -- rows 81, 82, 83, 84, 85 (reopened), 86, and 87 each carry a closure with a witness on metal, so no agent-closable red stands open and the constructive queue is clear.
think The running thread is the last lap's recommend line, and it named the crux exactly -- whether a supervisor that could be told which child finished, rather than choosing which to wait on, would convert any of the order-idle turns the eldest-first reaping leaves standing.
think Council rota by lap index -- 467 .kyri logs on disk, this is lap 468, and 468 mod 27 = 9, so the deep-read was foundations/20260810-011514_pond-the-application-module.md. Its shape governed the restraint here: Caravan supervises processes and stops exactly there, so this rung asks a scheduling question of one declaration rather than reaching toward policy that belongs to Pond.
think The ground does offer a wait-on-any -- a no-hang wait across the table, the same idiom subscribe_poll_service.rye already runs on metal -- so the limit rolling.rye named honestly is liftable rather than permanent, and the question became measurable.
think Reuse over restatement, three ways. cohort.spawn_one grew a spawn_one_extra that appends a caller's own words after the peers, so harvest hands each child a linger word without copying forty lines of spawn; rolling.admissible opened pub so the admission predicate is asked once in the tree rather than restated one reaping later; the held verdicts and head-of-line rule came over unchanged.
think Both readings sweep, and that is the design decision the numbers rest on. A reading that only blocked on the eldest could never see the sibling that finished first, so the waiting would go unmeasured rather than unspent -- sweeping under both makes eldest_held one number comparable across the two, and the eldest reading waits exactly as long either way.
think The linger had to be deliberate. Children of one document doing alike work finish in roughly spawn order, which is why eldest-first costs so little there and why the question is unmeasurable without a stagger -- so each item carries how long its child lingers, bounded at two seconds, and the child strips that word before delegating to the shared entry.
obs The finding came in two halves, and the second one is the one worth keeping: readiness converts a slot and never a domain. The head enters when a slot is free AND it is clear against every domain in the table, so reaping the ready child frees a slot sooner yet removes a domain only when that domain is the one the head was waiting on. Over the ordered lap the virtualiser conflicts with all three clients at once -- both readings stand idle the same two turns, and being told which child finished converts nothing at all.
obs Where the head waits on a slot the whole of it converts. One lingering eldest, and the age reading blocks on it while a sibling that has already exited holds the very slot the head is asking for: one idle turn by age, zero by readiness, five of six children reaped out of order, same document and same bound of three.
obs Both RED probes proven load-bearing on metal before the green was claimed. With the readiness choice reduced to the eldest's, the stagger check goes RED at one idle turn where a ready slot was waiting. With the linger set to zero, the same check goes RED -- alike children hand readiness nothing to reap early, which proves the stagger is the whole of the conversion rather than an ornament on it.
obs Two counters ordinary scheduling jitter can move by one -- the held-turn count and the out-of-order count -- stay out of the witness as pinned numbers. The selftest holds their load-bearing claims itself (a count of zero is RED there), and the witness reads the sentences, so the witness never reds on a healthy run.
obs Twenty-eight green-witnessed Rye rings now carry the Microkit shape across real processes. TAME surface GREEN -- style bans clean, width-check exit 0, and the neighbors this lap touched (cohort, rolling, concurrent) all re-GREEN after the shared spawn and the opened predicate.
loom witness=caravan_harvest ordered_idle_age=2 ordered_idle_ready=2 homed=6 peak_in_flight=3
loom witness=caravan_harvest stagger_idle_age=1 stagger_idle_ready=0 out_of_order=5 linger_ms=250 wall_ms_age=283 wall_ms_ready=259
file caravan/harvest.rye the rung -- two reapings of one queue, differing in which slot a turn takes, with the sweep that makes readiness knowable and the linger that makes it measurable
file tools/caravan_harvest_witness.rish the witness on metal -- both halves of the finding checked natively, jitter-bound counters left to the selftest
file caravan/cohort.rye spawn_one_extra, so a supervisor handing its children one word this module knows nothing of reuses the spawn whole
file caravan/rolling.rye admissible opened pub, so the admission question is asked once in the tree
file caravan/README.md the harvest section and roster row -- the doc moves in the same commit as the behavior it describes
file crux/REMEMBER.md live edge refreshed to this rung, the rolling edge condensed into the prior-edges line
recommend keep-going the honest next crux is what readiness cannot reach -- a head blocked on a domain waits for that domain and no reaping order shortens it, so the question becomes whether a supervisor may look past a blocked head at all, and what a document would have to declare for reordering to stay meaning-preserving rather than merely faster
